WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.838
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:03.838 --> 00:00:06.927
transient fields to separate saved object state

00:00:06.927 --> 00:00:10.081
from temporary state, then explain their default

00:00:10.081 --> 00:00:11.724
values after restoration.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:15.342
These skills let applications recover lasting

00:00:15.342 --> 00:00:18.247
records while giving temporary selections or

00:00:18.247 --> 00:00:21.417
observations a suitable starting state for a new

00:00:21.417 --> 00:00:21.945
session.

00:00:22.594 --> 00:00:25.448
A campus activity program gives Maya a score

00:00:25.448 --> 00:00:26.940
card with seven points.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.730
The owner and points should survive saving.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.584
A temporary view count starts at one when we

00:00:32.584 --> 00:00:35.503
construct the card; this small model does not

00:00:35.503 --> 00:00:37.644
automatically count screen views.

00:00:38.296 --> 00:00:41.031
Our report will compare the saved owner and

00:00:41.031 --> 00:00:43.703
points with the original and restored view

00:00:43.703 --> 00:00:44.148
counts.

00:00:44.148 --> 00:00:46.820
Then we will change the original count and

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.428
predict whether that changes the restored

00:00:49.428 --> 00:00:50.446
temporary value.

00:00:51.116 --> 00:00:53.749
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:00:53.749 --> 00:00:56.566
Main.java and build the score-card comparison.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.164
Add the familiar object input and output

00:01:03.164 --> 00:01:06.470
streams, Serializable marker, and Files and Path

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:07.021
imports.

00:01:07.021 --> 00:01:10.257
They support the same private object-file round

00:01:10.257 --> 00:01:12.185
trip as the previous lesson.

00:01:23.751 --> 00:01:26.745
Start ScoreCard with the fixed class version

00:01:26.745 --> 00:01:29.739
identifier, owner, points, and views fields.

00:01:29.739 --> 00:01:32.801
Mark only the temporary view count transient.

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:50.593
A transient instance field remains part of each

00:01:50.593 --> 00:01:53.435
object, but default serialization excludes its

00:01:53.435 --> 00:01:53.806
value.

00:01:53.806 --> 00:01:56.463
Owner and points are ordinary saved fields.

00:01:56.463 --> 00:01:59.243
All three are private; their different saving

00:01:59.243 --> 00:02:02.147
behavior comes from transient, not from private

00:02:02.147 --> 00:02:02.580
access.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.299
The static version identifier belongs to the

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:07.214
class rather than to each card.

00:02:07.233 --> 00:02:09.622
Complete the constructor and getters.

00:02:09.622 --> 00:02:12.528
The constructor accepts the owner and points,

00:02:12.528 --> 00:02:15.369
assigns those fields, and sets this original

00:02:15.369 --> 00:02:16.984
card's view count to one.

00:02:16.984 --> 00:02:19.696
Each getter simply reads its corresponding

00:02:19.696 --> 00:02:20.083
field.

00:02:44.663 --> 00:02:47.268
The assignment still happens during normal

00:02:47.268 --> 00:02:48.074
construction.

00:02:48.074 --> 00:02:50.928
Transient does not block assignment, clear the

00:02:50.928 --> 00:02:53.719
original field when writing, or make a getter

00:02:53.719 --> 00:02:54.339
change it.

00:02:54.339 --> 00:02:57.006
Later, default restoration of this ordinary

00:02:57.006 --> 00:02:59.363
serializable class will not replay its

00:02:59.363 --> 00:03:02.030
constructor or instance field initializers.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.263
A transient int then begins at zero.

00:03:04.291 --> 00:03:07.281
Add Main and its entry method, declaring the

00:03:07.281 --> 00:03:10.066
checked I/O and class-loading exceptions.

00:03:10.066 --> 00:03:13.192
Create a new private temporary file, start the

00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:16.249
outer cleanup structure, and construct Maya's

00:03:16.249 --> 00:03:18.016
seven-point original card.

00:03:46.213 --> 00:03:49.421
CreateTempFile creates an empty file and returns

00:03:49.421 --> 00:03:50.022
its Path.

00:03:50.022 --> 00:03:53.096
The dot bin suffix labels its name; the object

00:03:53.096 --> 00:03:55.034
streams determine its format.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:58.108
The throws declaration allows checked failures

00:03:58.108 --> 00:04:00.982
to leave main, while the outer finally will

00:04:00.982 --> 00:04:03.588
handle deletion of this run's own file.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:06.997
Open the object writer over byte output and pass

00:04:06.997 --> 00:04:08.660
original to writeObject.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.710
Close this resource block before opening the

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:12.195
reader.

00:04:35.029 --> 00:04:38.069
The representation includes Maya and seven from

00:04:38.069 --> 00:04:41.109
the ordinary fields, but excludes the transient

00:04:41.109 --> 00:04:41.821
view count.

00:04:41.821 --> 00:04:44.538
Writing that representation does not clear

00:04:44.538 --> 00:04:45.120
original.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.225
The original object still holds the one assigned

00:04:48.225 --> 00:04:49.454
by its constructor.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.605
Read the file through an object input stream and

00:04:52.605 --> 00:04:54.287
keep the result as Object.

00:04:54.287 --> 00:04:57.133
Check that it is a ScoreCard before casting.

00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:59.720
Then print the restored saved facts, the

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.437
original view count, and the restored view

00:05:02.437 --> 00:05:02.825
count.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:16.531
ReadObject reconstructs a distinct object using

00:06:16.531 --> 00:06:19.291
the compatible class and saved representation.

00:06:19.291 --> 00:06:22.172
The guard permits the specific cast and getters.

00:06:22.172 --> 00:06:24.873
The restored owner and points come from saved

00:06:24.873 --> 00:06:27.453
state, while its excluded int begins at the

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:28.594
field default zero.

00:06:28.594 --> 00:06:30.994
That differs from an uninitialized local

00:06:30.994 --> 00:06:33.575
variable, which Java requires you to assign

00:06:33.575 --> 00:06:34.235
before use.

00:06:34.358 --> 00:06:37.372
Finish the reader block and place deletion in

00:06:37.372 --> 00:06:37.908
finally.

00:06:37.908 --> 00:06:40.922
The reader closes before this fixture removes

00:06:40.922 --> 00:06:42.061
its private file.

00:06:42.061 --> 00:06:45.008
Now click Run to compare the actual objects.

00:07:03.378 --> 00:07:05.938
The report shows Maya with seven points,

00:07:05.938 --> 00:07:08.754
original views one, and restored views zero.

00:07:08.754 --> 00:07:10.354
The saved facts survived.

00:07:10.354 --> 00:07:13.235
The original was not cleared, and restoration

00:07:13.235 --> 00:07:15.923
did not repeat the ScoreCard constructor's

00:07:15.923 --> 00:07:16.627
assignment.

00:07:16.627 --> 00:07:19.059
The different counts follow our chosen

00:07:19.059 --> 00:07:20.403
temporary-field rule.

00:07:20.577 --> 00:07:22.914
For a new comparison, change only the

00:07:22.914 --> 00:07:25.883
constructor's view-count assignment from one to

00:07:25.883 --> 00:07:26.136
two.

00:07:26.136 --> 00:07:29.104
Keep transient on the field and retain the same

00:07:29.104 --> 00:07:31.252
owner, points, writer, and reader.

00:07:36.897 --> 00:07:39.985
Before running, predict all three output lines.

00:07:39.985 --> 00:07:42.876
Which object is constructed with the changed

00:07:42.876 --> 00:07:45.964
assignment, and does default restoration replay

00:07:45.964 --> 00:07:46.161
it?

00:07:46.161 --> 00:07:49.314
Pause here and explain the original and restored

00:07:49.314 --> 00:07:50.497
counts separately.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:01.252
Click Run to test that prediction with a

00:08:01.252 --> 00:08:02.790
complete new round trip.

00:08:09.158 --> 00:08:11.096
Maya and seven are unchanged.

00:08:11.096 --> 00:08:14.236
Original views is now two, while restored views

00:08:14.236 --> 00:08:15.105
remains zero.

00:08:15.105 --> 00:08:17.845
The changed constructor affects the newly

00:08:17.845 --> 00:08:19.582
constructed original card.

00:08:19.582 --> 00:08:22.723
It does not supply the restored temporary value

00:08:22.723 --> 00:08:25.930
because that constructor is not replayed by this

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:27.133
default mechanism.

00:08:27.297 --> 00:08:29.831
You separated lasting object facts from

00:08:29.831 --> 00:08:32.884
temporary state, then verified the original and

00:08:32.884 --> 00:08:33.924
restored values.

00:08:33.924 --> 00:08:36.263
A transient boolean follows the same

00:08:36.263 --> 00:08:38.212
field-default rule with false.

00:08:38.212 --> 00:08:41.265
Choose an excluded field only when its restored

00:08:41.265 --> 00:08:44.254
default fits the design; omitting a value does

00:08:44.254 --> 00:08:46.592
not automatically calculate a useful

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:47.372
replacement.

00:08:49.379 --> 00:08:52.073
In the notebook, compare keeping or removing

00:08:52.073 --> 00:08:54.890
transient, then build an equipment card with a

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:56.115
temporary selection.

00:08:56.115 --> 00:08:59.054
Which facts should that card retain, what should

00:08:59.054 --> 00:09:01.871
begin fresh, and how will your tests show both

00:09:01.871 --> 00:09:04.504
the saved values and the restored defaults?
